GNU-style NEWS file for crabbucket.  User-visible changes, newest first.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.3.0 (unreleased) [alpha]

** New features

  crabbucket-routes: a site that is a crate generates a `Route' enum from
  its content directory, so a link written in Rust stops compiling when
  the page it points at is renamed.  Two routes that would share a variant
  name fail the build naming both.

  It is also where `crabbucket' itself gets its answer to "what route is
  this file?", so there is one implementation rather than two that can
  disagree.  It has no dependencies and compiles in about a quarter of a
  second, which is what a build dependency should cost.

  examples/site-crate: a working one.

  RSS and Atom for a dated collection:

    [[feed]]
    collection = "notes"
    title = "Notes"
    limit = 20

  Pages gained an optional `date', which TOML parses as a real date.
  Configuring a feed is how a site says a collection is dated, so an
  undated page in one fails the build naming itself.  A feed needs
  absolute URLs, so a site with no `url' gets none and is told why.

  Social cards.  The default design system draws one per page from its
  own tokens, so a shared link is a title on the site's colours rather
  than a grey rectangle.  Nothing is configured; a site with a `url' gets
  them.

  crabbucket-og is a separate crate because it costs 76 transitive crates
  and a font, and a site with no interest in social previews should pay
  neither.  Cards are cached in `.crabbucket/', which takes a fourteen-
  page build from 0.37s back to 0.12s.

** Documentation

  A walkthrough for writing a design system, from an empty directory to
  twelve sites sharing one -- including what a version bump breaks and
  what it does not, which nobody writes down.

  Every snippet in it is from crabbucket-theme-plain, and a test asserts
  that the names it shows still exist.  A guide that quotes code goes
  stale silently, and one that no longer compiles teaches the wrong thing
  with confidence.

  A design system declares the frontmatter it reads, as `Theme::Extra',
  the way it already declares its layouts.  A page missing a field the
  design system requires fails when the file is read, naming it.  Before
  this a site could carry a field and the framework dropped it without a
  word -- which every page of a real migrated site did.

  `Page' is now generic over the theme rather than over its layout, so
  signatures gained nothing despite the frontmatter gaining a type.

  A site can add a page whose body it rendered itself.  It declares the
  page in site.toml under `[[page]]' and supplies the body through
  `Options::pages'; from there it is a page like any other -- navigation,
  sitemap, search, link checking, a social card, and the generated Route
  enum.  Declaring without rendering, rendering without declaring, and
  claiming a route the content already has are all errors.

  A site can register directives of its own, through
  `Options::directives', and a directive can read more than its own
  attributes.  `Directives::add_with' hands a handler a context carrying
  the configuration and the site's `data/*.toml' files, deserialized into
  the site's own types.  A lookup that misses fails the build at the
  directive's line.

  A name the design system already uses is an error rather than an
  override.

  `build_with' now takes its `Options' by value, because they carry
  directives, which cannot be borrowed out of a shared reference.

** API

  doc/STABILITY says what 1.0 will promise and what it will not, which
  types may grow without breaking, and where the design has no guarantee
  to offer.

  Error, Reason, Config, Options and Report are `#[non_exhaustive]'.  All
  five have gained members in every release so far, and deciding this
  after 1.0 would itself be the breaking change.

  `Options' is built with `Options::new()' and its methods rather than as
  a literal.  `Config::blank()' replaces `Config::for_tests'.

  Everything a caller must construct or receive is re-exported at the
  crate root, which an audit found untrue of eight types.  `DeadLink'
  gained a Display.

** Bug fixes

  A link to a route without a trailing slash -- `/docs/agents' rather than
  `/docs/agents/' -- was reported as a missing file.  It is a route, and
  every static host serves it.  Astro and Next both write links that way,
  so this was a tax on every internal link in a migrated site.

  `~/' in a link means the site root, whatever the base path is.  Content
  had no way to write a site-absolute link that survived the base
  changing: spelling one out hard-coded the base, and did so silently,
  because a link outside the base is not checked.  Only inside `href' and
  `src', so a shell path in prose is left alone.

  A design system that offered a search client but did not load it shipped
  a search box that could never appear.  crabbucket-ui now loads it, and
  the build warns when any client is written that no page loads -- the
  opposite of a dead link, and just as broken.

  The search index no longer swallows terminal captures and diagrams.  A
  fence marked `no-search' is rendered and highlighted as usual and left
  out of the index; anything carrying `data-search="off"' is skipped along
  with its contents.

** New features

  crabbucket-theme-plain: a second design system, deliberately unlike the
  first.  Light, serif, one column, one layout, no JavaScript.  It renders
  crabbucket's own documentation site unmodified.

  crabbucket-tokens: the token generator, and the contrast maths, as a
  build-time crate whose only dependency is `toml'.  Both lived inside
  crabbucket-ui until a second design system needed them.

  `Theme::router_js' and `Theme::search_js' return `Option<String>'.  A
  design system without a router says so, and a site that asks for one
  anyway is told once and still builds.

  The router fires `crabbucket:render' on first load and after every
  client-side navigation, so a design system with anything interactive
  hooks it rather than forking the router.  The built-in tab memory,
  contents highlighting and copy buttons go through the same event.



** API

  `build_with' and `Options' are re-exported at the crate root beside
  `build'.  They were reachable only as `crabbucket::site::*', so the
  symmetric name did not compile.

  `Report' implements Display, and says everything it carries: the page
  and link counts, any skipped drafts, and any warnings.  It also has
  `headline' and `drafts_line' for a caller that wants the warnings on a
  different stream.  Previously a build with something to say would have
  said it to nobody.

** New features

  `crab new --theme NAME' scaffolds a site that depends on a design
  system.  Such a site is a crate rather than a content directory, so it
  gets a Cargo.toml, a src/main.rs that calls `crabbucket::build', a
  turborust.toml that watches the Rust as well as the content, and a
  workflow that runs `cargo run --release'.

  The two shapes need different build commands and different watch
  inputs, and the content-shaped config in a crate-shaped site rebuilds
  nothing when the Rust changes.


** New features

  Markdown reaches typed components through directives:

    :::callout{kind = "warn", title = "Careful"}
    crabbucket needs Rust 1.85 or newer.
    :::

  Attributes are a TOML inline table and deserialize into the component's
  own props type, so a misspelled one fails the build with the file and
  the line.  A directive written inside a fenced code block is left alone.

  Seven components ship with the default design system: callout, cards,
  card, tabs, tab, steps and step.  The tabs use no JavaScript at all.

  A table of contents, built from the same heading list that fragment
  links are checked against.  Pages with fewer than three headings get
  none.

  A light palette, declared in design/tokens.toml under `[color.light]'
  and `[syntax.light]'.  No component changed to gain it.  Contrast is
  asserted by a test rather than judged by eye.

  Pages gained an `order' field, which sets reading order within a
  section: the sidebar, and the new previous and next links.  It is
  separate from `nav_order', which decides membership of the masthead.

  `search = true' builds a search index and ships the client that reads
  it.  The index is the pages, scanned linearly, which for tens of pages
  beats parsing an inverted index.  Past 300KB the build says so.

  With `router = true', the router now also moves focus into the new
  content, announces the new title, honours fragments, restores scroll
  position on Back, highlights the contents entry in view, adds copy
  buttons to code blocks, remembers a chosen tab, and prefetches on hover.

** Corrections

  The router is 2.9KB gzipped, not the "about a kilobyte" claimed since
  the first commit.  The number is corrected in README, doc/DESIGN and on
  the site, and a test now holds it to a budget.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (unreleased) [alpha]

** New features

  `crab new' scaffolds a site that builds with no edits: a configuration
  file, three pages, a turborust.toml for the dev loop, and a GitHub Pages
  workflow.

  `crab build' takes --out and --base, so a preview never means editing a
  tracked file.

  Content is Markdown with `+++' TOML frontmatter, deserialized into a type
  rather than a map.  A file's path below content/ is its route.

  Headings get stable ids and a permalink anchor.  An explicit {#id} wins
  over the derived slug.

  Code fences are highlighted while the site is built, into classes whose
  colours come from design/tokens.toml.  Nothing is sent to the browser to
  make code coloured.

  Design tokens are generated into both Rust constants and CSS custom
  properties from one file, so the two cannot drift apart.

  Layouts are an associated type on the theme, so a layout that does not
  exist fails the build listing the ones that do.

  Component styles are declared beside their components and namespaced, so
  two design systems may both have a `card'.

  content/404.md becomes 404.html.  Because it is served in place of every
  missing path, a relative link on it fails the build.

  sitemap.xml and robots.txt are written when site.toml has a url, and
  skipped entirely when it does not.

  An optional client-side router of about a kilobyte swaps <main> and
  drives the View Transitions API, and degrades to ordinary navigation.

** Build failures

  Every internal link is resolved after rendering and checked against the
  routes, the assets, and the target page's headings.  Every dead link is
  reported at once, each naming the page that wrote it.  There is no flag
  to disable it.

  Frontmatter and configuration errors show the offending line with a
  caret under it, and report the file-absolute line number.

  Exit status is 0 on success, 1 if the site is wrong, 2 if the command
  line is.

** Documentation

  A man page, doc/crab.1.  A test asserts that its options and those in
  `crab --help' are the same set.

** Notes

  The design is described in doc/DESIGN, and three of its sections have
  been revised by building the thing.  What is planned is in ROADMAP.md.

  Still unbuilt, and described as such: a generated Route enum, Markdown
  directives, a table of contents, and search.

  There is no dev server, and there will not be one.  Run `turborust up'.
